A simple web infrastructure consists of one server that hosts a website reachable via www.foobar.com.

The domain name foobar.com is configured with a DNS record that points to the server’s IP address (8.8.8.8). The "www" record is a type A record, which maps the domain name to an IP address.

When a user types www.foobar.com into their browser, the request is sent over HTTP/HTTPS using TCP/IP to the server.

The server contains:
- A web server (Nginx)
- An application server
- Application code
- A database (MySQL)

What is a server:
A server is a physical or virtual machine that provides services, resources, or data to other computers (clients) over a network.

Role of the domain name:
The domain name provides a human-readable address that maps to an IP address.

Type of DNS record:
The "www" record is an A record.

Role of the web server:
The web server (Nginx) handles incoming HTTP requests and serves static content or forwards requests to the application server.

Role of the application server:
The application server processes the business logic and generates dynamic content.

Role of the database:
The database (MySQL) stores and retrieves application data.

Communication protocol:
The server communicates with the user’s computer using HTTP/HTTPS over TCP/IP.

Issues with this infrastructure:

SPOF (Single Point of Failure):
If the server goes down, the entire website becomes unavailable.

Downtime during maintenance:
If the server needs to be restarted (e.g., deployment), the website will be unavailable.

Scalability issues:
This infrastructure cannot handle high traffic because all requests go to a single server.
